This module fixes a reference leak in the PLZ110 vendor signal observer.
Target: oplus_sys_stability_helper.ko from the tested device's vendor_dlkm.
Vendor ELF SHA256: 574d81868eec62ca5a83368887d4b4ad6665404586147099224980cf442c7792
Vendor ELF build ID: 4be64d767fc521a59f11c65e5a86eda5721264ba
Kernel: 6.12.69-android16-6-g242e1a07f878-ab15865446-4k, qcom,canoe

Observed and disassembled ownership path in send_signal_catcher (size 0x21c):
+0xcc calls get_task_pid(group_leader, PIDTYPE_PID), acquiring a reference.
+0xd8 calls pid_task(), borrowing the task and discarding the PID pointer.
The callback never releases that PID reference. Signalling a non-leader runc
thread therefore retains its group leader's PID and the owning PID/user
namespaces after every process has exited. QEMU without this vendor callback
correctly reclaims the namespaces and cannot reproduce the device-only leak.

The fix changes only the +0xd8 BL target to a helper which calls pid_task(),
releases the owned PID, and returns the same borrowed task. The callback's RCU
read-side section protects that task. Signal delivery, vendor classification,
logging, and all other callback instructions are preserved.

All 135 instructions must match the recorded fingerprint after masking only
ELF relocation immediates. Both get_task_pid and pid_task call targets are
validated, including the standard ARM64 module PLT. An out-of-range replacement
branch or any mismatch is rejected before patching. The single instruction is
written under stop_machine. The vendor module is retained and this module has
no unload path, so the replacement text remains valid until reboot.

Build: scripts/build-ddk.sh TARGET=compat OUT=/module/out/compat -j4
Native private imports require ksud insmod. Validate target BTF before loading.
The fix prevents new leaks; it does not force-release previously leaked PIDs.
Reboot once to clear the old references.

Regression: tests/vm/prepare-oplus-fixture.py and tests/vm/run-oplus-pidref.sh.
The generator consumes the exact vendor ELF and emits an offline fixture with
the same callback instruction layout. Unrelated private vendor routines become
no-op stubs; the fixture registers no hooks. Its C driver only invokes the
observer on a live non-leader thread. The tests prove the original leak, reject
a one-instruction mismatch, then verify 64 corrected calls and PID/user
namespace reclamation. The vendor ELF itself is not distributed in this repo.
